#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138314 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4b91363-1cac-5495-b29b-c14236bc4de6" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator"}] :question/id #uuid "638af342-1995-5403-84bd-b6c720cb432d" :question/math-academy-id "e-907" :question/problem "A guitar string, made of carbon steel, has a linear mass density of $3\\,950$ milligrams per meter. What is the linear mass density in grams per meter?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{mg}$ with $\\textrm{g}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{mg},$ we can put $\\textrm{mg}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{g},$ we can put $\\textrm{g}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\textrm{mg}} }{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{\\cancel{ \\textrm{mg} } } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{mg} }{\\textrm{m}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\textrm{mg} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{m}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The density given in the problem statement is $3\\,950 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{mg}}{\\textrm{m}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ milligrams per gram. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{1\\,000}$ grams per milligram.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,950\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{m}} & =3\\,950\\,\\frac{\\text{mg}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{mg}} \\\\  & =(3\\,950\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000})\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{mg}}}{\\text{m}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{mg}}} \\\\  & =3.95\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{m}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the linear mass density of the string is $3.95\\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{m}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138315 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e8464-e555-5358-b97f-230933822fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "08aa3547-cb51-5d00-8cb7-3e1a9bd4a695" :question/math-academy-id "e-908" :question/problem "Sand runs through a sand timer at a rate of $120$ grams per minute. What is this rate in grams per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}\\cdot\\, \\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\textrm{min}$ with $\\textrm{s}.$ To get rid of $\\textrm{min},$ we can put $\\textrm{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\textrm{s},$ we can put $\\textrm{s}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\require{cancel}\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\cancel{\\textrm{min}}}\\cdot \\dfrac{ \\cancel{ \\textrm{min} } }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nTherefore, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{g} }{ \\textrm{min}}\\cdot \\dfrac{  \\textrm{min}  }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $120 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{g}}{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ seconds per minute. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\dfrac{1}{60}$ minutes per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}120\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}} & =120\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{60}\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =(120\\cdot \\frac{1}{60})\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{g}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the sand runs through the sand timer at a rate of $2 \\,\\textrm{g}/\\textrm{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138316 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b82abac-b8b5-5e9e-bac1-38cc4f4de6c0" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator and a Denominator"}] :question/id #uuid "79f9c66b-2e4e-53ad-8146-10a2aa4e730d" :question/math-academy-id "e-909" :question/problem "A peregrine falcon can reach a speed of $360$ kilometers per hour during flight while diving. What is this speed in meters per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\textrm{km}$ to $\\textrm{m}.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}} }{\\textrm{hour}}\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}} } \\cdot \\,\\dfrac{?}{?} = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\textrm{hour}$ into $\\textrm{s}.$\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}} }{ \\cancel{\\color{red}\\textrm{hour}} }\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\cancel{\\color{blue}\\textrm{km}} } \\cdot \\dfrac{ \\cancel{\\color{red}\\textrm{hour}} }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{black}\\textrm{km} }{ \\color{black}\\textrm{hour} }\\cdot \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\color{black}\\textrm{km} } \\cdot \\dfrac{ \\color{black}\\textrm{hour} }{ \\textrm{s} } = \\dfrac{\\textrm{m}}{\\textrm{s}}\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $360 \\, \\dfrac{\\textrm{km}}{\\textrm{hour}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer.\n\n- The number of seconds in one hour is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1\\,\\text{hour} & =60\\,\\text{minutes} \\\\  & =60\\cdot 1\\,\\text{minute} \\\\  & =60\\cdot 60\\,\\text{s} \\\\  & =3\\,600\\,\\text{s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTaking the reciprocal, we find that there are $\\dfrac{1}{3\\,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}} & =360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot 1\\,000\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{km}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600}\\,\\frac{\\text{hour}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =(360\\cdot 1\\,000\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600})\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{km}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =100\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of the falcon is $100$ meters per second."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516830 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4b91363-1cac-5495-b29b-c14236bc4de6" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "15f65945-b500-528c-b740-17a557b9ff0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516155} {:answer/id #uuid "492377d5-2038-59ab-be78-068c1d4f8b93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516260} {:answer/id #uuid "8b9a5554-46d2-5bd3-9bac-5ac0beea217a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.015\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516403} {:answer/id #uuid "a3a5523c-6f55-59d2-b7c2-6c598b3d19c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001\\,5\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516451} {:answer/id #uuid "aab27a45-ad45-576b-b764-0f80ee6bf45e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.15\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516470}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "a3a5523c-6f55-59d2-b7c2-6c598b3d19c9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.001\\,5\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516451} :answer-field/id #uuid "ecc56b39-eec2-58a2-8d1d-910e05bec8f7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516757}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "a4020071-b095-5022-af4b-4598557e943b" :question/math-academy-id "q-105547" :question/problem "The acceleration of a cyclist is $1.5\\,{\\text{m/s}}^{2}.$ What is the cyclist's acceleration in ${\\text{km/s}}^{2}?$" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{m}$ with $\\text{km}.$ To get rid of $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{km},$ we can put $\\text{km}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{m}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The acceleration given in the problem statement is $1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.$\n\n- There are $1\\,000$ meters per kilometer. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{1\\,000}$ kilometers per meter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}} & =1.5\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{m}} \\\\  & =1.5\\cdot \\frac{1}{1\\,000}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{m}}}) \\\\  & =0.001\\,5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{{\\text{s}}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the acceleration of the cyclist is $0.001\\,5\\,\\text{km}/{\\text{s}}^{2}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516772 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e4b91363-1cac-5495-b29b-c14236bc4de6" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "917086dd-8ad2-56fb-8eb0-55b0aeef4f7e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97\\,200\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516417} {:answer/id #uuid "9ff5a488-9441-5023-926c-ce3a0b335fed" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "97.2\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516441} {:answer/id #uuid "b3f79363-79cd-5503-9bee-b923c291154b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.972\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516485} {:answer/id #uuid "f3bd8d2d-544f-5f51-b969-f8bb0251dbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9\\,720\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516616} {:answer/id #uuid "f638ad08-eeb3-554a-98df-a3d74479967d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.72\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516626}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f638ad08-eeb3-554a-98df-a3d74479967d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "9.72\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516626} :answer-field/id #uuid "4da99ea6-5345-5314-8a2c-a1800552e160" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516679}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0d475207-225c-5dcb-85b4-165f52b26314" :question/math-academy-id "q-105551" :question/problem "A cruise ship on the high seas travels at a speed of $972$ centimeters per second. What is the speed of the ship in meters per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{cm}$ with $\\text{m}.$ To get rid of $\\text{cm},$ we can put $\\text{cm}$ in the denominator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{m},$ we can put $\\text{m}$ in the numerator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed given in the problem statement is $972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters per meter. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters per centimeter.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}} & =972\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}} \\\\  & =972\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{s}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}) \\\\  & =9.72\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the speed of the cruise is $9.72\\,\\text{m}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516803 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e8464-e555-5358-b97f-230933822fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2526bd24-622c-5111-ad94-fbb56ead3d23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.01\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516183} {:answer/id #uuid "49972733-8d9d-5321-b836-cf1a178c9f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516261} {:answer/id #uuid "5ec01a81-fb61-582d-ae8d-2125920edf15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.6\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516316} {:answer/id #uuid "b523d36c-1bf0-53cc-978b-b71319d7a12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516489} {:answer/id #uuid "c3e8dd65-936c-5ce5-b9fd-88eb040a28a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "10\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516527}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b523d36c-1bf0-53cc-978b-b71319d7a12d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.1\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516489} :answer-field/id #uuid "45a8bede-a863-52ca-9cb6-ae8ed026f9dd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516676}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5082eabf-27a1-5373-b336-872133f4c0e0" :question/math-academy-id "q-105556" :question/problem "A racecar can reach a speed of $360$ kilometers per hour. What is this speed in kilometers per second?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{h}$ with $\\text{s}.$ To get rid of $\\text{h},$ we can put $\\text{h}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{s},$ we can put $\\text{s}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The speed given in the problem statement is $360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}.$\n\n- There are $3\\,600$ seconds per hour. Taking the reciprocal, there are $\\frac{1}{3\\,600}$ hours per second.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}} & =360\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{h}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600}\\,\\frac{\\text{h}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =(360\\cdot \\frac{1}{3\\,600})\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{h}}}{\\text{s}} \\\\  & =0.1\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{s}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the race car reach a speed  of $0.1\\,\\text{km}/\\text{s}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516809 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f10e8464-e555-5358-b97f-230933822fa1" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "311a3504-e36b-5f10-892e-bdeb105186c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "600" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516216} {:answer/id #uuid "462d5be7-6aa9-513b-9a51-6ade4b44dd82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1\\,500" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516255} {:answer/id #uuid "7f967064-3dce-5f42-b291-550af58a18c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516382} {:answer/id #uuid "ae284407-e445-5a54-b789-f40aae94b7e9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "60" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516474} {:answer/id #uuid "f0e752a9-62d5-5350-8ec9-a86a3355e5cb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516610}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7f967064-3dce-5f42-b291-550af58a18c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516382} :answer-field/id #uuid "16da39d2-fdd0-5aa5-84cd-d52ae5dab2c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516659}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "642f1676-b974-5c20-b33e-f45029a01f5a" :question/math-academy-id "q-46697" :question/problem "A car travels at a speed of $2.5$ kilometers per minute. What is this in kilometers per hour?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.$ To figure out what we need to multiply by, we can set up an equation to help us:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nWe want to replace $\\text{min}$ with $\\text{hour}.$ To get rid of $\\text{min},$ we can put $\\text{min}$ in the numerator so that it cancels out. To introduce $\\text{hour},$ we can put $\\text{hour}$ in the denominator.\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}}=\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes in one hour.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}} & =2.5\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{min}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}} \\\\  & =2.5\\cdot 60(\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{min}}}{\\text{hour}}) \\\\  & =150\\,\\frac{\\text{km}}{\\text{hour}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138828 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b82abac-b8b5-5e9e-bac1-38cc4f4de6c0" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator and a Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1e2988fa-d18c-5b90-ac1c-561062dc8688" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "175\\,\\text{min/week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516168} {:answer/id #uuid "53097714-c01c-56b3-be37-c288fe7f3d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210\\,\\text{min/week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516285} {:answer/id #uuid "92fc4243-c171-52b5-801c-0bafb7621011" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "150\\,\\text{min/week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516419} {:answer/id #uuid "afc05660-fc2c-5907-a9a0-b2ebe8087619" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "160\\,\\text{min/week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516478} {:answer/id #uuid "ea00352a-797f-5364-8b6d-067ce9cbb891" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "250\\,\\text{min/week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516598}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "53097714-c01c-56b3-be37-c288fe7f3d0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "210\\,\\text{min/week}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516285} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d620be5-872f-567c-b4eb-13ab7a596c28" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186135916}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "e4e12086-1e27-5f26-877c-16c074270288" :question/math-academy-id "q-52042" :question/problem "Lewis spends $0.5$ hours per day studying. How many minutes per week does Lewis study for?" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{hours}$ to $\\text{min}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hours}}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{s}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{day}$ into $\\text{week}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hours}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{week}}=\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{week}}=\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}}.$\n\n- There are $60$ minutes per hour.\n\n- There are $7$ days per week.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}} & =0.5\\,\\frac{\\text{hours}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot 60\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{hour}}\\cdot 7\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{week}} \\\\  & =(0.5\\cdot 60\\cdot 7)\\,\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{hours}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{min}}{\\cancel{\\text{hour}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{week}} \\\\  & =210\\,\\frac{\\text{min}}{\\text{week}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, Lewis studies at a rate of $210\\,\\text{min/week}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186516861 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186138444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0b82abac-b8b5-5e9e-bac1-38cc4f4de6c0" :knowledge-point/title "Performing a Unit Conversion: Converting a Numerator and a Denominator"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "086d4688-33de-5a64-866f-902df4384ebc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.9$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516128} {:answer/id #uuid "4d872133-f4d9-5e17-b850-ddc1193b671a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$8.1$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516270} {:answer/id #uuid "6f915334-6b87-5751-ad38-2d634085831f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.8$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516359} {:answer/id #uuid "cd64ecf1-525c-5508-8649-19fa1da0870a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$6.5$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516545} {:answer/id #uuid "fd3dad55-53b9-5149-9af5-5e3b2c18ac2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.3$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516644}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "fd3dad55-53b9-5149-9af5-5e3b2c18ac2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "$7.3$ meters" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516644} :answer-field/id #uuid "3a0b5d40-1768-5377-8893-9229f2f0b027" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186516672}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e856700f-73de-59c0-8d3a-1f40df24d089" :question/math-academy-id "q-52044" :question/problem "A tree sapling grows by $2$ centimeters per day. Assuming that one year has $365$ days, how many meters does the tree grow in one year?" :question/worked-solution "We are given the units $\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}},$ and we want to convert this into $\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.$ We're converting two units instead of just one, so we're going to have to multiply by two conversion factors:\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nOne of the conversion factors should change $\\text{cm}$ to $\\text{m}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\,\\frac{?}{?}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nThe other conversion factor should change $\\text{day}$ into $\\text{year}.$\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{year}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}\n$$\n\nSo, our conversion formula is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{year}}=\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.\n$$\n\nNow that we have our conversion formula, we need to determine the correct numbers to use, and apply it.\n\n- The rate given in the problem statement is $2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}.$\n\n- There are $100$ centimeters in one meter, therefore, there are $\\frac{1}{100}$ meters in one centimeter.\n\n- There are $365$ days in one year.\n\nApplying our conversion formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}} & =2\\,\\frac{\\text{cm}}{\\text{day}}\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{cm}}\\cdot 365\\,\\frac{\\text{days}}{\\text{year}} \\\\  & =2\\cdot \\frac{1}{100}\\cdot 365(\\frac{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}{\\cancel{\\text{day}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{m}}{\\cancel{\\text{cm}}}\\cdot \\frac{\\cancel{\\text{days}}}{\\text{year}}) \\\\  & =7.3\\,\\frac{\\text{m}}{\\text{year}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $2$ centimeters per day is equivalent to $7.3$ meters per year. In one year, the tree grows by $7.3$ meters."}]}
